```


`tricycle` infers cell cycle position by projecting each cell's gene expression into a 2D reference embedding by using pre-calculated PCA loadings. When cycling cells are present, this results in a continuous "loop" of cells around an origin. The origin can be specified manually or estimated from the data. Cell-cycle position is then calculated with reference to the origin, giving `tricycle_theta` which takes values $[0, 2\pi)$ in radians (i.e. 0 to 360 degrees).

## Expression around theta

For known cell cycle markers, expression is averaged across cells in angular bins around the origin. The right panel showes these averaged expressions as a function of theta. The lines are circular LOESS smooths of bin means, weighted by their cell counts. Colours group the canonical markers by their expected cell-cycle phase; CDKN1A (gene encoding p21 which acts as inhibitor of cyclin-dependent kinase complexes stopping cell cycle progression at the G1/S and G2/M transitions) is shown neutrally as a checkpoint diagnostic.

The left panel provides the corresponding tricycle projection coloured by theta. Grey radial lines mark tricycle's approximate phase boundaries: G1/G0 wraps from $1.75\pi$ through zero to $0.25\pi$, G1/S spans $0.25\pi$-$0.5\pi$, S spans $0.5\pi$-$\pi$, and G2/M spans $\pi$-$1.75\pi$. The grey vertical lines mark the same boundaries on the expression plot (right panel).

```{r expression_curves, fig.height=6, fig.width=14}
plot_cell_cycle_expression(report_data)
```

